const NARRATION_TEXT = "You don't need to ration your questions. <pause 0.3s> You need to shrink what Claude re-reads. Six habits do almost all of the work. One. Start a fresh chat between unrelated tasks. <pause 0.3s> The slash-clear command wipes the backpack right back to empty. A brand-new question costs a fraction of that same question buried at message twenty-five. Two. Scope to the file or module you're actually touching. <pause 0.3s> Point Claude at one place instead of letting it wander the whole codebase. Three. Share only the relevant code. <pause 0.3s> The one function, not the two-thousand-line file it happens to live in. Four. Plan before you build. <pause 0.3s> Agree on the approach in a sentence or two first, so Claude doesn't explore ten dead ends on your budget. Five. Combine related requests into one. <pause 0.3s> \"Add the field, update the test, and fix the type\" in a single ask re-reads the context once, not three times. Six. Check what you're carrying. <pause 0.3s> The slash-context command shows exactly what's filling your window, and slash-compact summarizes a long session so it stops re-reading the raw history. Hardly anyone runs either one \u2014 and they're the two that show you the leak and then plug it. Or you can skip the reading altogether. <pause 0.3s> A code-graph tool \u2014 like the open-source CodeGraph \u2014 pre-builds a map of your whole codebase, so Claude queries one graph instead of grepping through thirty files. <pause 0.3s> In real tests that cut tokens by nearly half, and up to sixty-four percent on a huge repo. Six habits. <pause 0.3s> But if you only ever adopt one, there's a single habit that beats the other five combined. <pause 0.5s> And it's the one almost nobody uses.";
